model_response:
Certainly! Your current bubble sort implementation always iterates over the entire range, even after the largest elements have "bubbled up" to their correct positions. We can optimize this by shrinking the right boundary on each pass, since the end of the range becomes sorted after each outer iteration.

**Optimized Bubble Sort:**

- On each outer iteration, reduce the upper bound (`end`) by one: the last element is now in place.
- The inner loop should only go up to the last unsorted element.

Here’s how you can apply this in your existing code structure:

```cpp
template<typename RandomIter, typename Comparator = std::less<>>
void bubble(RandomIter begin, RandomIter end, Comparator comp = Comparator{}) {
    using difference_type = typename std::iterator_traits<RandomIter>::difference_type;

    std::cout << "start" << std::endl;
    std::cout << std::distance(begin, end) << std::endl;

    if (std::distance(begin, end) == 0) { return; }

    // Last element that needs to be sorted
    for (RandomIter unsorted_end = end; unsorted_end > begin + 1; --unsorted_end) {
        bool swapped = false;

        // Go up to one before the current unsorted end
        for (RandomIter current = begin; current < unsorted_end - 1; ++current) {
            RandomIter next = current + 1;
            if (comp(*next, *current)) {
                std::swap(*next, *current);
                swapped = true;
            }
        }

        // Optional: Break early if no swaps were made (already sorted)
        if (!swapped) break;
    }
}
```

**Key Improvements:**
- The inner loop only bubbles up to the *current boundary* (`unsorted_end - 1`), so already sorted elements at the end are not revisited.
- The optional `swapped` flag lets you exit early if no exchanges were made in a pass, further optimizing for nearly-sorted input.

**In Summary:**
- Instead of looping to `end - 1` on every inner pass, the right boundary shrinks.
- This reduces unnecessary work, especially for largely sorted sequences.

